<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Os Lusíadas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as The Lusiads</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Os Lusíadas (The Lusiads) is the national epic of Portugal, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1572. In ten cantos of ottava rima it tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1497–1499 and, through it, the history of the Portuguese people, mixing classical gods with recent events. It is the most celebrated work of Portuguese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas.jpg" alt="Os Lusíadas" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Os_Lus%C3%ADadas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Os Lusíadas, usually translated The Lusiads, was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís Vaz de Camões</a>, who lived from 1524/5 to 1580, and first published in 1572. It is written in the Homeric fashion and is frequently compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s Aeneid. It was composed while Camões was an exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>. The heroes are the Lusiads, the sons of Lusus, that is, the Portuguese. He dedicated it to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian of Portugal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The ten cantos are in decasyllabic ottava rima, with the rhyme scheme ABABABCC, and total 1,102 stanzas and 8,816 lines. The narration begins in stanza 19 of Canto I, in medias res, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s fleet already past the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. The gods watch over the voyage: Venus favours the Portuguese, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, associated with the east, resents the encroachment on his territory and is opposed to them.</p><p>In Canto III Vasco da Gama tells the king of Melinde the history of Portugal, from Lusus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a> through the first dynasty of kings, with episodes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ourique">Battle of Ourique</a> and the tragic story of Inês de Castro. Canto IV carries the story through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota">Battle of Aljubarrota</a> to the sailing of the fleet, when an old man on the beach, the Old Man of the Restelo, speaks pessimistically of the venture. In Canto V the giant Adamastor appears to the sailors. The fleet reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in Cantos VII and VIII, and in Canto IX the sailors rest on the Isle of Love, prepared by Venus, where sea nymphs meet them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In Canto X the goddess Thetis, now the lover of Gama, prophesies the future of Portuguese exploration and conquest, including the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a>, and shows him a vision of how the Ptolemaic universe works. She also tells of the voyage of Magellan. The poem ends with further advice to the young King Sebastian. The arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the most important part of the poem, was placed at the point that divides the work by the golden section, at the beginning of Canto VII.</p><p>The work is widely regarded as the most important work of Portuguese-language literature and as Portugal's national epic, much as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> was for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Os Lusíadas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portuguese culture produced the Manueline style of the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém, the epic <b>Os Lusíadas</b> of Luís de Camões, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and cartography.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Renaissance humanists identified Portugal with Lusitania, and Luís de Camões called his national epic of 1572 <b>Os Lusíadas</b>, ‘the Lusiads’, after the mythical Lusus; ‘Lusophone’ still describes the Portuguese-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
